# 11. RTL / LTR ARCHITECTURE

Build a consistent directionality architecture.

## 11.1 Document Attributes

Prepare the shared layout to resolve:

```html
<html lang="ar" dir="rtl">
```

for Arabic, and:

```html
<html lang="en" dir="ltr">
```

for future English routes.

The current Arabic output must remain unchanged.

Do not activate English rendering globally during Phase 10.

## 11.2 CSS Logical Properties

Audit existing shared styles.

Prefer logical properties such as:

- `margin-inline-start`
- `margin-inline-end`
- `padding-inline`
- `border-inline-start`
- `border-inline-end`
- `inset-inline-start`
- `inset-inline-end`
- `text-align: start`
- `text-align: end`

Do not perform an uncontrolled global CSS replacement.

Identify actual direction-dependent defects.

For any existing physical left/right usage, determine whether it represents intentional positioning or directional layout before changing it.

## 11.3 Directional Icons

Determine which icons must reverse in RTL and LTR.

Examples:

- Back.
- Next.
- Previous.
- Directional arrows.
- Breadcrumb arrows.

Do not reverse non-directional icons:

- Search.
- Shopping bag.
- Heart.
- User.
- Check.
- Shield.

Reuse the existing Icon system.

Do not add a new icon library.

## 11.4 Typography

Preserve Arabic font rendering.

Prepare future English typography using existing font infrastructure where possible.

Avoid adding fonts or dependencies during Phase 10.

Do not introduce positive letter spacing into Arabic text.

## 11.5 Layout Testing

Verify current Arabic pages after adding localization groundwork.

Use an isolated LTR test context where practical.

Do not publicly expose incomplete English pages merely to test directionality.

